Date: Mon, 22 Nov 1993 10:38:28 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: BlackNet Investigations Needs More Detailed

tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May) writes:
>BlackNet Investigations, Inc., is pleased with the many revelations of
>drug use by database subjects here, but is distressed that so many of
>the revelations are anecdotal and second-hand.

Indeed. 

I attended Russ Whitaker's conference last weekend (the First European
Conference on Computers, Freedom, and Privacy) and it's left me
feeling a little bit paranoid.

It is worthwhile to note that whatever you send over this list is almost
certainly being logged.  (There are relatively few international tcp/ip
links, and I'd be willing to bet that, like the Telex system, they're
spooled by local government sigint organizations.)

(If you review my posting on drugs you will note that the related
incidents were disclaimed as partly or entirely fictional.  And no, I'm
not going to reveal which.  Certainly not over email.)

Moreover I'm not entirely willing to bet my freedom on the security
offered by encryption.  Encrypted messages travel in full sight, and
GCHQ/NSA have some very big computers and some very bright
cryptographers.  It's like waving a day-glo pink placard in public
saying I'M HIDING SOMETHING.

Nor am I willing to bet on concealed networks.  Traffic analysis, at
least in the UK -- with only one major telecoms network that carries
80%+ of all phone traffic -- should trivially reveal any network
dependencies to a security agency once they have a single node to
monitor.

The only solution to this problem is a network that is invisible; that
doesn't _look_ like a network to the phone tappers, that doesn't
disclose the participating sites to traffic analysis if the spooks
identify one, that doesn't reveal its contents to decryption if they
find a site and somehow begin monitoring it. As far as I know, no such
network exists ... although it is in principle possible.

If you want to talk to me about this, bung me your pgp public key at
my home address (charlie@antipope.demon.co.uk) and expect an encrypted
reply in a day or three. But don't expect me to talk about it in a
public place. 


-- Charlie


