Date: Mon, 22 Nov 1993 11:29:53 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: Tech: Truth about Canon Copiers

Harry Hawk writes:

>A common thread a month or two ago was about what happens when you
>try to copy a US Currency with a Canon color copier.
        :
>Clearly their is something in the scan (input) function of the copier
>that is preventing the bill from being copied.

>I suspect it looks for the "color of money" and if it finds it, it 
>does further checks for US bills.

Which just means you need to be ingenious.

Most modern electronic products are localized to their market. For
example, machines marketed for the US are modified for use in the UK
-- different mains voltage, frequency, TV broadcast standards, and so
on. There's obviously some kind of money recognizer in the scanner of
the copier. It may well be armed to scan for all the _major_
currencies ... but if you look hard enough I'm sure you'll find a
species of foreign banknote that it doesn't recognize. Of course you
then have the problem of getting the special paper banknotes are
printed on (for example, British currency has a woven metal strip
running through it, just to make copying harder), but this is not an
insuperable obstacle.

I'd be willing to bet that if you can come up with some of the right
kind of paper and a sample banknote you'd have little difficulty, for
example, copying whatever they use in Uzbekistan on a copier sold in the
US.  (Or even in programming a workstation to print copies with
sequentially changed index numbers.) Preventing forgery of Uzbekistani
banknotes is not a major concern for Canon, who probably sell few
copiers there and who are not under pressure from the US government to
prevent forgery of Uzbekistani currency on pain of being excluded from
the US market.  Your only problem is finding a bank to deposit the
notes in.  I wonder if advanced scams of this kind have yet made any
serious economic impact on third world countries who can't get
third-party manufacturers like Canon to defend their fiscal integrity?



-- Charlie
