Date: Mon, 22 Nov 1993 08:49:55 -0800
From: tribble@netcom.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Exit strategies

We hear lots of horror stories about VCs, but remember that the
horror stories are the vivid stories that bear repeating.  Lots of
investments happen without people getting screwed, and lots of
investments happen with people only thinking they'd been screwed ("the
investor wanted out so we're screwed so it's his fault for taking his
marbles away").

But, if you don't give investors a good exit strategy, they will find
one, and you probably won't like it.  They want near term ROI, which
can change the flavor of your company (they don't need a long-term
viable corporate culture, they need one that looks good to analysts,
for instance).  Also, VCs could play games like getting someone to come
in with them, letting the company develop the technology, but lose the
marketing, and then licensing the technology to another of their
startups that they own more of (thus the development cost is shared,
but they retain ownership of most of the technology).

If they know how they're going to get money from your company and
business plan, then these kinds of things don't have to happen.

dean

