From: Mark Sulkowski <SULKOM@ubvmsb.cc.buffalo.edu>
Subject: ANARCHY

 From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
>I was active in LP politics for a while, and we managed to get 20%
>of the vote for our state representative candidate.  This was the
>highest vote% for a Libertarian in the Pacfic Northwest.  The vast
>majority of Libertarian candidates get 0-5%.  But while relatively
>spectacular vs. other LP candidates, 20% is still nowhere close to winning.

	True, but it builds credibility.


>The California school choice initiative, similar to the one passed in
>Wisconsin, upon being vilified as a "libertarian" initiative by its 
>opponents, had its campaign funding dry up, and lost by a landslide, c. 
>70%-30%.

	Are you sure this was a causal connection?  (I'm not discounting
the possibility that it is.)

	I've also heard stories of Libertarian candidates getting elected
IN SPITE OF "hit pieces" that quote the LP Platform or make allegations
of terrible consequences of electing Libertarians.

	Given this, your evidence seems inconclusive to me.


>  Such a stunning rout is rare in the history of public 
>initiatives.  Even the much hyped "Libertarian" state legislators in New 
>Hampshire actually got elected as Democrats and Republicans, while also 
>carrying the LP label.

	Did you see that actual results?  More people than you might
suspect pulled the LP lever.  The candidates' affiliation with the LP
was never secret either.  At least people were willing to vote for them.
IMHO, you are insisting on seeing the negative side of things here.


>The LP has a long-standing reputation as a fringe whacko party, 
>running Hustler Busty Beauty centerfolds, punks, hippies, etc. who get 
>get the 1% fringe whacko vote, and nothing any idealists 
>have done for decades, and little we might do now, can change that.  

	I doubt that.  The New Hampshire contingent has been gaining
credibility and respect over there.  People are learning that it is
possible to TRUST Libertarians that are in office.  They can only learn
this if Libertarians can get elected, at least somewhere.


>I don't dismiss electoral politics as an important adjunct for
>our political efforts, but it is silly to make a process that
>has such little effect the main focal point of the efforts of
>so many well-meaning political idealists.

	I'll agree with you here.


>In answer to
>the original question, some of us have found much more 
>effective ways to bring our own world closer to anarchy[...]

	One objection that I have to the strategy you suggest
is that it relies on encouraging people to be criminals.  Tax
resistors and hackers have a bad enough stigma as it is.  While
crytography might make it much easier to cheat the laws, it
might seem dirty enough that it will never achieve wide spread
use, and who knows, the government could try to crack down on
people who do use it no matter what they are using it for.
It seems to me that this could be a very rocky road to anarchy.

